  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Stepping Stones with Children aged 5-14 years</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will develop, test for the first time, produce, and introduce a training package, &#x2018;Stepping Stones with Children&apos;. Parents and carers find it hard to discuss HIV with young children. &#x2018;Stepping Stones&apos; is a proven popular training package used in over 100 countries and available in 25 languages.  It helps adults and young people explore how relations between men and women, age and other issues influence the way people behave, and how behaviour can be improved. Currently, Stepping Stones does not include skills to help people relate to younger children aged 5-14 on issues like death, disclosure of HIV status, stigma and age-appropriate information on HIV and sex. The Stepping Stones approach uses peer groups working alone at first, then coming together in a larger mixed group, to share their ideas about particular problems, their causes, and how people can work together to address the problems. The training takes place over a three to four-month period. This application is recommended for funding. The project will create a training package to work with young children (aged 5-14) who are living with and affected by HIV. &#x2018;Stepping Stones with Children&apos; will give children, their families and those caring for them the materials and skills to better relate to each other on sensitive and difficult issues like disclosure, death, stigma, and HIV.</narrative>
  </description>
